<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#konohiki">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konohiki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A konohiki was the headman of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahupua-a">ahupuaʻa</a>, a land division of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, who administered the land on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> chief who ruled it. The islands were split into districts, or moku, running from the mountain tops down to the sea, and these into ahupuaʻa, named after the boundary altars where taxes were collected during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makahiki">Makahiki</a>. Usually a relative of the chief and of aliʻi rank, the konohiki oversaw water rights, the distribution and farming of land and its upkeep, and saw that gifts and tributes reached the chief at the right times. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> entered the kingdom, the konohiki became tax collectors, landlords and wardens of the fisheries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Each island kingdom, and after Kamehameha I’s conquests of 1795–1810 the whole chain, had one ruler, who shared out the land among the chiefs and their agents (<b>konohiki</b>).</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
